.TH DIRNAME 1
.SH NAME
dirname \- return the directory portion of a pathname
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dirname
.RB [ \-z | \-\-zero ]
.I string
.RI [ string ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B dirname
utility deletes the trailing
.B /
character and the last component of
.IR string ,
writing the remaining directory portion to the standard output.  If
.I string
contains no
.B /
characters,
.B dirname
prints
.RB \(dq . \(dq
(the current directory); a
.I string
that is just
.RB \(dq / \(dq
yields
.RB \(dq / \(dq.
.PP
For source compatibility with the POSIX, BSD and GNU variants,
.B dirname
accepts one or more operands; each is processed independently and its
directory portion is written on its own line.  This matches BSD when a
single operand is given.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-zero
Separate output entries with a NUL byte rather than a newline.
.TP
.BR \-\-help
Print a usage message and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.PP
Option processing stops at
.B \-\-
or at the first operand; unlike GNU, options are not permuted past an
operand (BSD/POSIX behaviour).  A lone
.B \-
is treated as an operand.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B dirname
exits 0 on success and >0 if an error occurs.
.SH EXAMPLES
.B dirname /usr/bin/sort
.br
/usr/bin
.PP
.B dirname stdio.h
.br
.
.PP
.B dirname /
.br
/
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR basename (1),
.BR readlink (1),
.BR dirname (3)
.SH STANDARDS
The
.B dirname
utility is compatible with POSIX; the multiple-operand and
.B \-z
extensions follow GNU coreutils.  Where BSD and GNU disagree, BSD
behaviour is preferred.
